+### The flight a painting has or has not
+
+**A promoted wall carries its own staircase.** `promote-backdrop.mjs` writes `meta.stairs` from
+`stairsForFacing` — the identical shape `deriveMeta` bakes, `raw_w`/`raw_h` and all, so the
+renderer, `meta.stairs_list` and the ledger read a promoted flight exactly as they read a derived
+one and nothing learns a second vocabulary. Row 32's refusal is retired rather than softened: it
+existed *because* nothing attached a flight, and with the attachment there is no longer a way for
+the tool to write a meta that loses one. Ten manor walls could not be painted at all while it stood,
+however good the painting.
+
+**It is projected at the promoted meta, not at the derived one**, because the promoted meta is what
+the page draws this wall with: the scale, the floor line and the corners the painting fixed are the
+terms `groundplane` puts the flight on the frame with, and a flight projected at any other camera
+lands beside its own painted treads. On `library/E`'s own doctored case the two differ by 212 px of
+body and 96 px on the first tread nose. On a **snapped** wall (`row35_snap.py`) and on one promoted
+through the **declared** camera the projection is exact by construction — the snap rectifies the
+picture onto the declared camera and writes the corners, floor line and scale that camera states,
+and the declared route takes its horizon from `deriveMeta` itself — so on those routes the flight
+the meta carries and the flight the plan projection draws are the same numbers, not close ones.
+
+**Exactness is a fact about the geometry and says nothing about the paint.** A meta can be perfectly
+right about where the flight stands and the pixels under it still be bare plaster, which is a click
+travelling through a staircase that is not in the picture. So two questions are asked of every
+promotion on every camera source, and they are separate clauses because different evidence answers
+them and they cost different things:
+
+| clause | evidence | what it costs to be wrong |
+|---|---|---|
+| `row32:stair.flightless_ask` | the PROMPT beside the candidate, which must carry `frame-language.FLIGHT_ASK`'s own mark | a re-ask of a wall whose painter was never told about the stair — which is the re-ask the content-gap grant exists to give |
+| `row32:stair.painting_has_no_flight` | the PICTURE, read by `tools/flight-in-frame.mjs` | a re-ask of art that was right |
+
+**The ask arm is row 29(a)'s reasoning applied to a staircase**: the ask is checked, not the picture.
+Every manor stair wall on disk was painted from a prompt that never mentioned one, so whatever
+stands in that region got there by accident while the meta says where the *building* puts it — those
+two agreeing is luck. `frame-language.mjs` asserts the mark against its own sentence as it writes
+it, so the emitter refuses itself rather than leaving the promotion hunting a sentence nobody writes.
+
+**The picture arm is a rank, not a threshold.** A painted flight is a stack of parallel lines: its
+edge energy concentrates ACROSS the tread noses and starves the direction ALONG them, so
+`E(across)/E(along)` taken over the flight's projected body should stand near the top of the same
+ratio taken at all 180 orientations. On bare board or plaster the grain has its own direction, which
+has nothing to do with where the plan puts this staircase. What is reported is the RANK, which under
+*this region holds no flight* is uniform — a p-value by construction, with no magnitude to calibrate
+against a corpus that will keep changing. The region is the flight's own body (`treads_poly` ∪
+`mass_poly`), never `poly`: the hull covers the bare wall behind a rising flight and the bare floor
+in front of a descending one, which is most of it, and every statistic taken over the hull was
+diluted to nothing.
+
+**The labelled corpus, and what it says the arm can and cannot do.** Ten manor walls whose plan draws
+a flight, each labelled by eye off the projection drawn over the frame:
+
+| | rank |
+|---|---|
+| frames that DO paint a flight in the region | 0.061 0.094 0.111 0.150 0.411 |
+| frames that do NOT | 0.439 0.489 0.711 |
+| bodies too small to read (withheld) | `back_stair_head/W`, both rolls, 29,766 px |
+
+The cut is `RANK_LEVEL`, the median, **because that is the boundary the null itself names** — not
+because it is where this corpus splits, which is 0.43 and would be eight points of tuning. So the
+arm refuses the frame whose flight lands on bare plaster over bare floor (`back_stair/E`) and lets
+two further bare frames through, whose projected bodies are narrow strips of floorboard whose grain
+happens to run across the noses. **It is deliberately one-sided and the separation is not clean**;
+a sharper instrument is a row, not a threshold move. Where the body on frame is under
+`MIN_READABLE_BODY_PX` the reading WITHHOLDS and the promotion attaches — the same shape as every
+other WITHHELD in this project, a refusal to speak rather than a finding.
+
+**Why the reading is in the promotion and not in the measurement.** `door_measure.py`'s rule — the
+reading goes in the measurement — protects two things and this breaks neither: nothing computed here
+reaches the meta (the output is a yes or a no), and the answer is a pure function of the candidate's
+bytes, whose sha256 the measurement already pins, so `fixtures.spec`'s staleness re-run answers
+identically or the image changed. What could not be done is put it in the measurement: the region it
+asks about is the region **this promotion's own geometry** projects, and a reading taken before the
+promotion exists cannot name it. A door's void is found without the plan; a flight's region is
+nothing but the plan.
+
+**The decoder is checked against one nobody here wrote.** `readLumaPng` is ~60 lines of PNG defilter
+over `zlib.inflateSync`, because this repository has no runtime dependencies. A misread frame would
+change every number the instrument produces and nothing downstream would notice, so `flight.spec`
+decodes a shipped backdrop in Node and in the browser and compares a coprime grid of samples.
+
